WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.583
The model generated a query.

00:00:04.583 --> 00:00:07.167
It runs.

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:09.750
But does it answer the question?

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:12.333
You trust generated SQL the way you trust any code: with tests.

00:00:12.333 --> 00:00:14.916
And software gives you three.

00:00:14.916 --> 00:00:17.500
A unit test proves one piece is right.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.083
A regression test proves it stays right.

00:00:20.083 --> 00:00:22.666
A library lets you build on what is already trusted.

00:00:22.666 --> 00:00:26.172
Start with one query.

00:00:26.172 --> 00:00:29.678
You already know the answer you expect, so you check that the query reproduces it.

00:00:29.678 --> 00:00:33.185
And a query is a stack of exact operations.

00:00:33.185 --> 00:00:36.691
A schema, then joins and filters, then a group-by and count, then the result.

00:00:36.691 --> 00:00:40.197
Each layer fixes a piece of the meaning, so each layer is one thing you can test.

00:00:40.197 --> 00:00:43.703
Did the right rows survive, the Pluto case?

00:00:43.703 --> 00:00:47.209
Did it count the right thing?

00:00:47.209 --> 00:00:50.716
Did the number match what you expected?

00:00:50.716 --> 00:00:54.222
You settle each by running the query, not by arguing in English.

00:00:54.222 --> 00:00:57.728
That is a unit test.

00:00:57.728 --> 00:01:01.884
Trace the first reading, the LEFT JOIN, on one page.

00:01:01.884 --> 00:01:06.041
First, every play joined to its artist.

00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:10.197
Mickey, Minnie, and Daffy each played a Taylor song, but each also played the Beatles.

00:01:10.197 --> 00:01:14.353
Then the WHERE clause drops the Taylor rows.

00:01:14.353 --> 00:01:18.510
And here is the catch: every user still has a row left, a Beatles play, or Pluto with no song at all.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.666
So after the group by, all four users survive.

00:01:22.666 --> 00:01:26.823
That is the bug.

00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:30.979
Query A keeps people who did hear Taylor, just because they also heard something else.

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:35.104
Now trace the second reading, the NOT IN, the same way.

00:01:35.104 --> 00:01:39.230
The subquery finds everyone who heard Taylor.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:43.355
Mickey, Minnie, and Daffy all did.

00:01:43.355 --> 00:01:47.480
Then NOT IN drops exactly those three.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.606
And only Pluto is left, the one user who never heard Taylor at all.

00:01:51.606 --> 00:01:55.731
Same English as before, two queries, two different answers, and this is the one the spec actually asked for.

00:01:55.731 --> 00:02:00.254
A unit test answers one thing: is this query right, today.

00:02:00.254 --> 00:02:04.777
Keep it, and re-run it on every change, and it answers a harder question.

00:02:04.777 --> 00:02:09.301
Is it still right.

00:02:09.301 --> 00:02:13.824
The day someone quietly redefines what active user means, the stored test fails that day.

00:02:13.824 --> 00:02:18.347
Not months later, after the dashboard has already lied to a hundred people.

00:02:18.347 --> 00:02:22.581
And a verified query is a function.

00:02:22.581 --> 00:02:26.815
Once you have proven that non Taylor listeners returns just Pluto, you build the next query on top of it, and you check only the new layer.

00:02:26.815 --> 00:02:31.049
Verified queries compose into a library of trusted SQL.

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:35.282
That library is the semantic layer.

00:02:35.282 --> 00:02:39.516
You could never do this in English.

00:02:39.516 --> 00:02:43.750
You cannot run a sentence, re-run it, or build a bigger query on top of it.

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:47.628
So that is verification.

00:02:47.628 --> 00:02:51.506
A unit test for one query, a regression test so it stays right, and a library you compose on.

00:02:51.506 --> 00:02:55.383
The model writes the query.

00:02:55.383 --> 00:02:59.261
Deciding whether that query is right stays your job, and that job is SQL.
